INSERT INTO documents (url, title, content, metadata) VALUES ('pdf://the-pleadings-game', 'Thomas F. Gordon, The Pleadings Game – An Artificial Intelligence Model of Procedural Justice', 'Artificial Intelligence and Law 8: 255–264, 2000. © 2000 Kluwer Academic Publishers. Printed in the Netherlands.  255  Book Review  Thomas F. Gordon, The Pleadings Game – An Artificial Intelligence Model of Procedural Justice, Kluwer Academic Publishers, Dordrecht, 1995. The term Computational Dialectics first appeared, to my knowledge, when Loui & Gordon organized an AAAI workshop by that name in Seattle, in 1994. In the same year, Gordon’s Artificial Intelligence and Law article was subtitled ‘an exercise in computational dialectics’. Gordon claims that The Pleadings Game “is a contribution to Computational Dialectics” (p. xi). Although Gordon is one of the founding fathers of this sub-field, I believe The Pleadings Game is more than that: it is a contribution to Law, AI, and AI & Law. At least everyone working in the field of AI & Law should be familiar with this great work. In the early nineties, at the time the Artificial Intelligence and Law community started to become interested in the representation of legal argumentation by means of dialogues, Thomas Gordon completed his formalization and implementation of a dialogical model of legal argumentation: the pleadings game. In 1995 Tom Gordon’s dissertation was published by Kluwer Academic Publishers.1 Because The Pleadings Game2 (henceforth: TPG) had not been reviewed so far in Artificial Intelligence & Law, I thought it was about time to do so. The structure of TPG is clear. After an introductory chapter, the legal domain is explained in chapter 2. In chapter 3 and 4 theories of legal philosophy and formal systems of argumentation are evaluated, respectively. Central to the TPG is chapter 5, in which a “normative model of pleading, founded on first principles, inspired by Robert Alexy’s discourse theory of legal argumentation” (p. 112) is developed.3 1 Just as other AI & Law books afterwards (e.g., Hage, 1997; Prakken, 1997; Royakkers, 1998;  Lodder, 1999) The Pleadings Game was meant to appear in the Law and Philosophy Library, but due to internal miscommunication at the publisher’s side it was not. 2 Reference to the book are in italics (The Pleadings Game), to the game itself in normal font (the pleadings game). 3 The heart of TPG is Section 3 (called the Pleadings Game) of Chapter 5 (called the Pleadings Game). In that section the pleadings game is defined.  256  BOOK REVIEW  This model, called the pleadings game, is meant to shed some light on the following questions (p. 1): • What are the limits of judicial discretion? • How should it be decided whether these limits have been respected? • Are there methods for constructing decisions which are sure to fall within these limits? Chapter 6 describes in main lines, but with enough detail “to allow any AI programmer to reimplement the game” (p. 170) the implementation of the pleadings game in Standard ML. TPG “presents a formal, computational model of adversarial legal argumentation, the pleadings game, whose purpose is to support, illustrate and clarify some fundamental theoretical claims about the nature of legal reasoning and argumentation” (p. 10). The claims are analytical (“to understand judicial discretion, legal procedures must be modeled as language games”), empirical (“Article Nine of the UCC consists of defeasible legal rules of various kinds”), and normative (“deductive theory of justification is wrong”). In what follows I will discuss the chapters Philosophy of Legal Reasoning (Chapter 3), Formal Models of Argumentation (Chapter 4) and especially the definition of The Pleadings Game (Chapter 5). In my concluding remarks I will briefly address the above claims of Gordon and his contribution. 1. Philosophy of Legal Reasoning In Chapter 3 the theories of Hart, Rödig and Alexy are discussed and evaluated. In research on AI and legal reasoning Hart’s work is/was central (p. 21), reason why it is addressed in TPG. Hart is well-known for his distinction between easy and hard cases. According to Hart, a case is easy if straightforward rule application (by subsuming facts under the condition of a valid legal rule) is possible, otherwise it is hard. A judgement in an easy case is correct if the rules are applied properly. Judicial discretion is solely restricted to hard cases: the judgment in a hard case is always correct. Gordon decides not to build upon the theory of Hart, because it “does not provide a satisfactory definition of a correct or justified legal judgment” (p. 30). Easy cases can become hard ones, for instance the literal meaning of a clear rule can be disputed using teleological arguments. However, Hart’s theory does not take into account these types of arguments. From 1950 onwards researchers became interested in applying formal logic to law in order to describe law and legal reasoning in a precise manner. Rödig is a representative of this ‘legal logicians’-stream.4 Rödig’s prime concern was to axiomatize the law rather than formalize it (for a discussion I refer to TPG). I want 4 Some of the contempary AI & Law research (by Gordon, Loui, Prakken, Sartor, etc.) can be  considered as a continuation of this stream, and is even referred to as legal logic (Verheij, Hage & Lodder, 1997).  BOOK REVIEW  257  to address an interesting argument against demanding deductive proof that Gordon raises while discussing Rödig’s theory (pp. 49f.). Assume one party interprets a statute to mean p → q, and the other party believes it to mean r → q. If both parties agree that p and r took place, than q holds no matter which interpretation is chosen. However, to derive q deductively, one of the interpretations should be chosen. Gordon prefers to use an elliptical argument instead and justify q solely by the set {r, p}. I fully agree with him and argued elsewhere (e.g., Lodder, 1997) that this type of justification can be modeled adequately in a dialogical model. Interestingly enough, this example cannot be modeled in the pleadings game, because in Gordon’s game arguments have to include the rule the argument is based on. Alexy’s theory is discussed in length (pp. 53–73). An instructive summary of the main theories Alexy builds upon is provided (Habermas, The Erlangen School, Perelman). An important conclusion that Gordon derives from Alexy is that “discretion should not be limited by the untenable line between hard and easy cases, but by fair rules of legal procedure” (p. 72; see also Hage, Leenes & Lodder 1994). Gordon’s main objection is that Alexy’s theory is general (while the law knows various different procedures) and too vague. Both drawbacks are dealt with in his definition of the pleadings game. In the final section (3.4) dimensions of discourse games are described, but, in my opinion, the section is not appropriately located. Because only some of these dimensions were discussed in Chapter 3, it would have been better to start chapter 4 (on formal models of argumentation) with this section. This brings us to . . .  2. Formal Models of Argumentation The work of Lorenzen, Pollock, Simari & Loui, and Geffner & Pearl is discussed “not so much to assess their suitability for modeling legal argumentation as to gather ideas and techniques which can be adapted in a discourse game designed expressly for this purpose” (p. 76). Although the discussion of the models is clear, I could not find (neither in this, nor in the next chapter that defines the pleadings game) the explicit mentioning of the techniques and ideas that are borrowed from the described systems. Also, why Gordon chooses to use conditional entailment is not totally clear, since the models of Simari & Loui and of Geffner & Pearl seem both qualified for the task: “[t]hey provide the central techniques necessary for handling defeasible legal rules” (p. 106). In chapter 5 Simari & Loui are not considered anymore, but it is stated that “Geffner & Pearl’s system is attractive for several reasons . . . ” and “the question arises whether it is possible to somehow encode explicit exceptions using specificity. The answer is yes” (p. 115). As much a reason as its suitability I presume that Geffner & Pearl’s conditional entailment was chosen because the logic was brand new (as was Simari & Loui’s) at the time of writing. So Gordon was not only very up-to-date, he was also capable of incorporating a complicated new logic in his pleadings game. Let us turn now to that influential game . . .  258  BOOK REVIEW  3. The Pleadings Game The formal definition of the pleadings game consists of 9 definitions (definition 7–15; definition 1–6 concern the language for explicit exceptions), 10 rules about allowed moves, a bunch of functions (mainly discussed on pp. 128–129), and a recursive procedure in psuedo code that formulates the rules for pleading (p. 141). The informal explanations are concise and there is only one, extended example that does not illustrate the definition of the game explicitly, but presents a session of the implementation. It is therefore hard to obtain full insight into the game. It took me quite some time before fully understanding the pleadings game, but I do not regret to have spent the time. I learned a great deal about legal argumentation and procedural, dialogical models, and learned how to play an interesting game. Moreover, I believe that I have managed to explain the working of the pleadings game in a clear way. While discussing my interpretation of the pleadings game (3.1–3.4) the following topics will pass review: how to play the game, the concepts relevancy and issues, the representation of rules, and some suggestions for improvement. 3.1.  PLAYING THE PLEADINGS GAME  The pleadings game starts after the plaintiff has filed his complaint. In this complaint, referred to as the main claim, the plaintiff determines the subject of the dialog. The first move in the dialogue is the defendant’s reaction to the main claim. The defendant can either concede or deny the main claim. The pleadings game ends if the defendant concedes the main claim, but normally he will deny. Denying means that he wants further evidence for the statement, that is, he wants his opponent to justify the statement. After the defendant has denied the main claim, the plaintiff has to adduce an argument supporting his complaint.5 An argument is a set with the following content: 1. a formula saying that some particular rule is valid; 2. formulas corresponding to the (instantiated) condition of this rule; 3. a formula saying that this rule is applicable. By applying a (valid) rule an argument supporting the conclusion of the rule is created. If the plaintiff has made his argument, it is the defendant’s turn once more. In reaction to the argument of the plaintiff the defendant has to perform each of the following assertions in his next turn. (i) He must either deny or concede that the rule is valid. If the validity of the rule is conceded, the validity becomes indisputable. If the validity of the rule is denied, in the next move the plaintiff will have the opportunity to adduce an argument supporting the claim that the rule is valid. 5 The plaintiff can also deny the denial. In that case the pleadings game ends with only one issue:  the main claim. Surely, the plaintiff will know arguments that support his claim, because otherwise he would not have started the Game.  BOOK REVIEW  259  (ii) He must concede or deny the instantiated condition(s) of the rule. The consequences of these assertions are the same as above (i). (iii) He must concede the argument, or adduce a counterargument. Conceding has the same consequence as above (i). Note, however, that if an argument is conceded, it does not necessarily imply that the conclusion of the argument follows. This is only the case if there is also agreement about both the validity of the rule and the instantiated condition of the rule. The other possibility is to adduce a counterargument. The counterargument is composed of exactly the same elements as the argument described above. The conclusion of the counterargument is opposite to the conclusion of the argument. The process of both parties conceding and denying statements, and exchanging arguments (counterarguments, countercounterarguments, etc.) continues until each statement is either: (a) conceded (b) twice denied (c) countered If a statement is conceded, the parties agree about the statement, making further discussion of the statement unnecessary. If a statement is denied, and the other party denies this denial, there is no need to proceed any further. Otherwise, meaningless yes/no ’discussions’ would be the result. When a statement is twice denied, the statement has become an issue that will not be further discussed in the pleadings game, but is left for trial in court (The Trial Game).6 Finally, a statement can be countered. A denial (e.g., not p) is countered by an argument supporting the statement (p) that was denied; an argument is countered by a counterargument. If each statement is reacted to in the above (a–c) described way, the pleadings game ends, because then there are no statements left open for discussion. Consequently, a player’s turn is the last of the game if he has introduced no new statements. This is the case if: 1. no argument is adduced;7 2. no statement is denied, except denials.8 In the pleadings game only one reaction to each statement adduced by the opponent is allowed. Although in terms of costs and time this is a good policy, there is also a drawback: the outcome of a debate may be opposite to the outcome if more reac6 Gordon defines (pp. 152–155) the Trial Game in which all issues of the pleadings game are  decided. All issues of the pleadings game are either conceded or denied. It is only defined that the issues have to be decided upon, not how. The game is a single player game in which no arguments, no grounds are asked for, so the game is not really an interesting one. 7 Each introduced argument brings with it at least a new statement that expresses the argument. 8 If a statement is denied, a new statement, viz. the denial, becomes part of the game. That a denial may be denied follows from what is said about statements which are denied twice (see b).  260  BOOK REVIEW  tions were allowed. For instance, a player who by mistake produced an argument that is defeated loses the conflict, although he might have more strings to his bow. The game is played against background knowledge that is normally empty at the beginning of the game, but can also be filled with formulas and rules agreed upon in a pre-pleadings game negotiation, which is “some unspecified procedure” (p. 141). 3.2.  RELEVANCY AND ISSUES  During the pleadings game the players are not allowed to assert anything they like. The proponent of the move can perform one or more of the following moves: 1. forced – react to relevant open statements of the opponent; 2. optional – react to open statements of the opponent that are not relevant; 3. optional – declare (an arbitrary number of) rules. A statement is relevant if it is about an issue. The four different statements the game knows, (claim p), (denial(claim p)), (argument A p) and (rebuttal A p C), are relevant if p is an issue. In order to explain how issues arise and expire, I introduce the notion of a decisive argument or rebuttal. An argument (rebuttal) is decisive if the opponent: • conceded the argument (rebuttal), and; • either conceded the backing of the rule of the argument, or is committed to this backing because of a decisive argument supporting it, and; • either conceded the conditions of the underlying rule, or is committed to the conditions of the underlying rule because of a decisive argument supporting it. Because each statement may be reacted to only once, it is impossible that a conceded argument is also countered. So in case of a decisive argument, which is an argument that is conceded, there can be no counterargument.  If the opponent concedes a claim, the formula that the claim is about is no longer an issue; the formula p is justified. After a denial there are three different scenarios.  BOOK REVIEW  261  First, if the denial is denied, the formula remains an issue that has to be decided in the Trial Game. Second, if a decisive argument has been put forward, the formula p is justified. A decisive argument is not necessarily the argument put forward in reaction to the denial, but can also be adduced after one or more rebuttals. An argument has the following structure: (argument(An ∪ Rn−1 p)),9 where n ≥ 1. If n = 1 the first argument was successful, and there was no rebuttal; if n = 2 then the first rebuttal is successfully countered. In general, if n = k then the (k − 1) rebuttal is successfully countered. Third, is no decisive argument. What happens then depends on the opponent’s reaction. If the opponent is able to put forward a decisive rebuttal, it is established that the formula p cannot be justified. A rebuttal has the following structure: (rebuttal An p Rn ), where n ≥ 1. If n = 1 the first rebuttal was successful, if n = 2 the second rebuttal was successful. In general, the n-th argument is successfully rebutted. In case the argument was not rebutted, or no decisive rebuttal has been put forward, the formula p remains an issue. 3.3.  THE REPRESENTATION OF RULES  In law it is important that arguing about the validity of rules is possible. The pleadings game offers this opportunity. Gordon realizes this by adding as a condition to a rule that it is backed. However, in my opinion, Gordon’s solution is not completely adequate because not only the backing of the rule, but also the whole procedure of rule application is part of the conditions of the rule. The condition of a rule contains too much information. In my opinion, the validity or applicability of a rule should not be part of the condition of the argument, but should be modeled as separate statements that may be used to justify the argument. This is important, because in my opinion not all arguments have underlying rules (Lodder, 1997). Moreover, an argument without an underlying rule would make it possible to model the example against demanding deductive proof that was described above. In that case, namely, (the conclusion of) the argument can be accepted without it being necessary to accept the interpretation of the rule, while in the pleadings game the conclusion of an argument only follows if the underlying rule is agreed upon. The way Gordon models exceptions is inspired by Toulmin. In my opinion, the disadvantage of this representation is that exceptions occur in two different ways. First, the exception can be explicitly represented (unless) in the rule. Secondly, the exception can be modeled as a rule. if a then b unless e if e then not b 9 A stands for the set of formulas meant to support the formula p. R stands for the set of formulas  meant to rebut the argument.  262  BOOK REVIEW  A player will not easily represent a rule with an explicit exception. If he does, he gives away one way the argument can be attacked. He can better leave the exception out and wait for his opponent to come up with the exception in a different rule. 3.4.  SUGGESTIONS  I will now make some suggestions for possible improvements. Firstly, it seems to me that the move that supports a claim in response to a denial can be formulated more elegantly. An argument A that supports the formula p, stored as the statement (argument A p), is introduced in a somewhat unnatural way: defend (denial(claim p)) A).10 Arguments can be put forward in a way that is more isomorphic to how they are stored. For instance, instead of the above defend move, the argument could be put forward as: (assert(argument A p)). The conditions and consequences of all moves can remain largely unaltered. The replacement of defend with assert leads to the following changes. pleadings game (defend(denial(claim p)) A) (defend(argument An p) Rn ) (defend(rebuttal An p Rn )) (defend(rebuttal An p Rn ) B)  Suggestion (assert(argument A p)) (assert(rebuttal An p Rn )) (assert(argument Rn false)) (assert(argument An+1 p))11  A second point at which the rules of the game might be improved is that not all statements have to be conceded explicitly. In civil pleading a party that does not react to a statement becomes committed. Therefore, it would have been more in accordance with actual legal practice if the ‘silent implies consent’ principle had been used. An additional advantage would be that in that case a player has to perform significantly fewer moves. It seems that the number of statements a player must react to is already reduced by the use of the notion of relevancy which implies that a player only has to react to all relevant open statements. However, given the constraints of the game, it seems that irrelevant open statements hardly occur.12 For instance, in the detailed example described by Gordon at no stage of the game there are irrelevant open statements. A final suggestion concerns Gordon’s rule that if an argument is adduced, all formulas of the argument (except the applicability assumption) become claims that must either be conceded or denied. If a party knows one of them to be false, he has no opportunity to prove this immediately. Instead, he must wait for the proof 10 This move means that the claim p that has been denied by the opponent, is supported by the argument A. 11 Note that A n+1 = An ∪ Rn ∪ B. 12 In his comments on this review Prakken suggests that irrelevant open statements cannot occur. Although I do not believe he is right, in case he would be, the demand to react to relevant open statements would be redundant (relevant could be left out).  BOOK REVIEW  263  of his opponent. In my opinion, it would speed up the game if it would be allowed to adduce arguments against claims. It is rather artificial that a player who has evidence that a statement is not true, may not put forward his evidence immediately, but first has to ask his opponent to bear his burden to prove that the statement is true. 4. Concluding Remarks As promised, let us reconsider the empirical, analytical and normative claims Gordon made. In my discussion the empirical claim was not addressed, but undeniably article Nine of the UCC consists of defeasible legal rules (like most legal statutes, to my opinion). In this review I described an example to illustrate the normative claim: the deductive theory of justification is wrong. Finally, Alexy’s theory was used to back the analytical claim stating that in order to understand judicial discretion legal procedures must be modeled as language games. This latter claim fits in with the three questions concerning legal discretion Gordon wanted to shed light upon. Did he succeed? The first question is about the limits of judicial discretion, the second about how to decide whether these limits have been respected, and the third about whether methods exist for constructing decisions which are sure to fall within these limits. In Gordon’s view the discretion of the judge concerns the factual and legal issues not agreed upon in the pleadings game. It seems to me that Gordon is partially right here. As far as factual matters are concerned, the parties can determine facts by agreement. However, besides the issues the judge has to settle in the Trial Game, he should also be allowed to correct the parties if they interpreted the law wrongly. This latter point is not dealt with in the Trial Game. So, in my opinion, the discretion of the judge is too limited. Given Gordon’s limits of discretion, the rules of the pleadings game are well-suited to decide whether these limits have been respected (see my description on how issues arise and end/remain). As a consequence, a method exists for constructing decisions which are sure to fall within these limits: the procedural method defended by Gordon and implemented as the pleadings game. This is one of the main contributions of TPG. Gordon not only argues that procedural models of argumentation are essential in representing legal reasoning, he also demonstrated by his implementation how such a model works. Other major contributions of TPG are (not all addressed here) amongst other things: • the necessity of using nonmonotonic logic for modeling legal reasoning is demonstrated; • the possibility to limit judicial discretion by a fair procedure is shown; • a formal argument-based adversarial game for civil pleading has been developed; • the claim that in legal reasoning deductive proof is neither necessary nor sufficient is elaborated.  264  BOOK REVIEW  A last remark concerns the players of the pleadings game. Because of the difficulty of the underlying logic (Conditional Entailment), and the great number of statements a player has to react to in one turn, it takes almost super humans to play the game. It certainly takes time to learn the game. However, the ideas behind the Pleadings Game are valuable. And once one knows how to play the Pleadings Game, the game itself is valuable too. Despite the fact that according to Gordon the game is not intended to be really played (it has theoretical purposes), in my opinion, by playing the game one can learn a lot about legal reasoning. References Hage, J.C. 1997. Reasoning with Rules, An Essay on Legal Reasoning and its Underlying Logic, Kluwer Academic Publishers, Dordrecht. Hage, J.C., R. Leenes & A.R. Lodder 1994. Hard cases: a procedural approach. Artificial Intelligence and Law 2, 113–167. Lodder, A.R. 1997. Procedural Arguments, in: A. Oskamp et al. (eds.), Legal Knowledge Based Systems, JURIX: The Tenth Conference, JURIX ’97, GNI, Nijmegen. Lodder, A.R. 1999. DiaLaw – on Legal Justification and Dialogical Models of Argumentation, Kluwer Academic Publishers, Dordrecht. Prakken, H. 1997. Logical Tools for Modelling Legal Argument – A Study of Defeasible Reasoning in Law, Kluwer Academic Publishers, Dordrecht. Royakkers, L.M.M. 1998. Extending Deontic Logic for the Formalisation of Legal Rules, Kluwer Academic Publishers, Dordrecht. Verheij, B. 1996. Rules, Reasons, Arguments: Formal Studies of Argumentation and Defeat, Dissertation, Universiteit Maastricht. Verheij, B., J. Hage & A.R. Lodder 1997. Logical tools for legal argument: a practical assessment in the domain of tort, Proceedings of the Sixth International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Law, ACM, New York.  Computer/Law Institute Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam lodder@rechten.vu.nl http://www.rechten.vu.nl/∼lodder  Arno R. Lodder  ', '{}');